WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.000
The refrigeration cycle is the foundation of everything you'll do in this trade.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:40.000
The refrigeration cycle has four stages. Compression: the compressor takes
low-pressure vapor and compresses it into high-pressure high-temperature gas.
Condensation: hot gas flows through the condenser coil where outdoor air removes

00:00:40.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Here are the key terms. Write these down.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Superheat: Temperature of vapor above its boiling point at the current pressure.
Measured at suction line.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Subcooling: Temperature of liquid below its condensing point at the current
pressure. Measured at liquid line.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:00.000
TXV: Thermostatic Expansion Valve adjusting refrigerant flow based on evaporator
superheat.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Here's why this matters on the job: Every cooling diagnosis starts with the
refrigeration cycle. Understanding what should happen at each stage lets you
identify what is actually happening.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Watch out for these: High superheat means not enough refrigerant reaching
evaporator. And — Low superheat means too much liquid risking compressor damage

00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Before you move on — what happens to refrigerant in the evaporator? Don't look
it up. Think about it.

